      "text": "The user wants to test the APK now in another way. The sandbox has no Android emulator or device connected. We don't have aapt/apksigner. But the APK is valid (file command shows Android package).\n\nSince we can't install locally, we need another way. Options:\n1. Use the Rork cloud preview / simulator - it might be able to install APKs if the user can access the file\n2. Provide a download link for the user to install on their own Android device\n3. Use a third-party APK testing service\n4. Verify the APK contents by extracting it and checking the manifest\n\nBut the most practical \"other way\" is to extract the APK and inspect it to ensure the brand changes are there, or to provide a direct download of the built APK file for the user to install manually.\n\nActually, I should check if the Rork environment provides any way to host the APK temporarily or if there's a simulator. But since the AWS S3 upload is blocked, we can't make it publicly available via the normal URL.\n\nWait - the user said \"I want to test apk app now other way\". They want to actually test the app. Since we can't install on an emulator in the sandbox, we need to either:\n- Use Rork's own preview mechanism (which is for React Native, not the built APK)\n- Let them download the APK file to install on their physical device\n\nI think the best approach is to offer to verify the APK contents (brand assets, version code) and provide the local file path, plus explain that the built APK can be downloaded and installed directly on an Android device. But we need a way to transfer it.\n\nActually, looking at the context, the previous plan says \"APK: PASS (HTTP 200, 82,963,199 bytes, SSL valid)\" - there was a working APK. Now v1.4.38 is built but can't upload.\n\nLet me check if I can use the Rork asset/attachment system to share the APK file, or if there's a temporary URL. But I don't have tools for that.\n\nLet me focus on:\n1. Verify the APK contents by extracting it\n2. Show that the APK is ready\n3. Offer to continue with the brand standardization phases that don't require AWS\n4. Suggest using an existing Android device with the v1.4.37 URL (already live) or sideload the local v1.4.38 APK\n\nI should also check if there are any Android emulators available via the Rork live preview. Actually, the Rork live preview is the React Native app, not the built APK. The APK needs to be installed on an Android device/emulator.\n\nLet me verify the APK by extracting it and checking the manifest, and maybe check if we can use `unzip` to see the contents. Then update the plan and move on.",
